# # T10-L07 · Judges that agree with humans: calibrating an LLM-as-judge with bias probes and bias-corrected scores
#
# **You will build:** the instruments that turn "a model said A is better" into a number you can
# defend: a swap-order probe for position bias, a swap-resolved verdict, a length probe, and a
# bias-corrected win rate with an interval, all measured against pairs that humans labelled.
#
# **Time:** ~60 minutes · **Runs on:** a laptop CPU, no GPU, numpy only · **Prerequisites:** T10-L06-eval-power-and-ranks,
# T07-L03-evaluating-agents, P02-L06-review-queues-real-reviewers
#
# The judge here is a **scripted stand-in** with biases that this lesson plants on purpose, so every
# number is checkable against the truth. The lesson measures a *method*; it makes no claim about how
# any real judge model behaves.
#
# By the end you will be able to:
#
# 1. Implement a swap-order probe, and measure how often a judge takes the first slot in both orders.
# 2. Implement a swap-resolved verdict, and measure what it fixes and what it leaves in place.
# 3. Implement a length probe that compares the judge with humans on the same pairs.
# 4. Implement a prevalence-corrected win rate from the judge's measured sensitivity and specificity.
# 5. Implement a paired bootstrap interval for that win rate, and measure when it covers the target.

